{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cfb8zCcfPH9yvZQh929Ec","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've written the candidate, `leaky_memory_bayesian_randomness`, and it passes the admission self-check (prints `OK`). It is probably a weak model: its smoke-fit ELPD-LOO is about −3205, against −2391 for the best model in the set. Novelty, convergence and the full fit's speed haven't been checked yet; admission does those.\n\n**Hypothesis.** People judge randomness the normative way, by Bayesian model comparison. A sequence looks random to the extent a fair coin explains it better than the generators they might suspect: a biased coin, or a coin that tends to repeat or to switch. Neither generator's bias is known in advance, and both are equally likely beforehand. The one distortion is leaky memory: each flip counts for less the further it is from the end of the sequence. That makes a streak at the end count as stronger evidence of non-randomness than the same streak at the start. This targets the critique's only discrepancy, that people avoid sequences ending in a longer streak more than the best model predicts. That discrepancy is weak, though: it doesn't survive the false-discovery correction (p = 0.038, q = 0.221).\n\n**Model.** There is one memory-retention parameter and one decision sharpness, both shared by everyone. I also added a personal guessing (lapse) rate. That goes slightly beyond the brief's \"exactly one distortion\", but it is part of the response process rather than a second cue, and every strong model in the set has one.\n\n**Main reason it may fit worse.** Unlike the best model, it gives people no personal ideal switching rate, so it can't capture people disagreeing about alternation. So it tests the normative account plus recency, rather than trying to top the current best model.\n\n**Speed fix.** My first version took 5.4 ms per gradient evaluation. At the production settings (7,000 iterations per chain at target_accept 0.99), the full fit could have run past the 30-minute limit. I rewrote it to compute the Bayes factor once per distinct sequence instead of once per trial, which brought it to 0.26 ms.\n\n**Warning.** The smoke fit flagged PSIS-LOO as unreliable: 4.7% of trials have a high Pareto k, above the 1% tolerance. A one-chain, 100-draw fit can do that on its own, but it may come back in the full fit. If it does, the model's ELPD would be marked untrustworthy.\n\nFiles are in `/scratch/users/kushinm/auto-psych/outer_loop_live/run1/data/subjective_randomness/experiment2/model_loop/iter_0/candidate_0/`:\n- `hypothesis.md`\n- `model_name.txt`\n- `candidate.py`"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":734,"cache_read_input_tokens":78123,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":734,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d4b23353-a60d-49f8-b6d8-9fa6a0dd439f","uuid":"b472cfb5-0480-4c14-b77e-a092ce966838","timestamp":"2026-10-01T07:40:34.834Z","request_id":"req_011Cfb8zBXRSEa23Y9SRrgrm"}
